haikuporter: batch packageEntries' per-file dirname/mkdir/mv (issue 758)

Patches haikuporter, not a recipe. Applied by
`graviton/scripts/haiku-provision-native-builder` (section 2a-5) right after the
haikuporter clone -- anchored, idempotent, byte-compiled to verify, and FATAL on
a moved anchor. Guarded by `graviton/scripts/haiku-packageentries-selftest`.


WHAT IS WRONG

`HaikuPorter/ShellScriptlets.py:packageEntries()` is the shell helper every
recipe's INSTALL() uses to move built files into a subpackage's staging
directory. Upstream's loop forks three processes per entry:

	for file; do
		...
		targetDir=$(dirname "$packagePrefix/$file")
		mkdir -p "$targetDir"
		mv "$installDestDir$prefix/$file" "$targetDir"
	done

For the dozens or hundreds of entries a normal recipe packages that is
invisible. `app-text/texlive` hands it roughly 400,000 entries, so it is
~1,200,000 forks. Measured on the native arm64 builder at ~27 entries/s, the
phase needs about 7 hours. The observed run was SIGKILLed partway through, with
no output and no error message of its own -- which reads as a build failure
rather than as a loop that simply had not finished yet.

There is no recipe-side lever. This is haikuporter's own code, and a recipe
cannot batch a function it only calls. Same channel, and the same reasoning, as
the `#136`/`#47` haikuporter patches in this directory.


THE CHANGE

Three edits inside the loop, none of which alters what ends up where:

  * `dirname` -> `${targetDir%/*}`. No fork at all. Safe because
    `$packagePrefix` is absolute, so the string always contains a slash. The
    one input where the two spellings differ is a doubled slash (`a//b`), where
    the parameter expansion yields `.../a/` and `dirname` yields `.../a`; both
    name the same directory, so the only effect is one extra `mkdir -p`.

  * `mkdir -p` only when the target directory actually changes. Entry lists
    reach this function grouped by directory -- they come from `find`/glob
    output -- so in practice this collapses to one `mkdir` per directory.

  * `mv` batched per directory, flushed when the directory changes and capped
    at 256 entries. 256 x ~120 B is ~31 KB of argv, far under ARG_MAX, so a
    single flat directory holding 400k files cannot build one unbounded command
    line. `"${batch[@]}"` is used throughout, so names containing spaces or
    newlines are passed intact.

`lastDir` and `batch` are declared `local`. That is load-bearing, not tidiness:
`packageDebugInfos()` calls `packageEntries()` again inside its own loop, so
leaked state would flush a stale batch into the wrong directory on the second
call. `targetDir` is deliberately left non-local, exactly as upstream leaks it.


HOW IT WAS VERIFIED

Not by reading it. `haiku-packageentries-selftest` applies the real patcher to a
recorded copy of upstream `packageEntries`, then *runs both shell functions* --
upstream's and the patched one -- over the same entry list with counting shims
on `dirname`, `mkdir` and `mv`, and compares the results byte for byte. The
fixture is 648 entries covering deep nesting, a 600-entry flat directory (which
is what exercises the 256 cap), names with spaces, a literal `$`, an embedded
newline, a bare directory entry, an absolute-form entry, and a second
`packageEntries` call in the same shell to catch leaked loop state:

	ok   resulting package tree identical (648 entries)
	ok   what is left behind in installDestDir identical
	ok   stdout identical
	ok   stderr identical
	ok   exit status identical (0)
	ok   fork count cut by >10x: 1947 -> 24
	ok   zero dirname forks (was 649)
	     upstream: dirname=649  mkdir=649  mv=649  total=1947
	     patched : dirname=0    mkdir=11   mv=13   total=24

81x fewer forks with byte-identical placement. The test also proves the patcher
aborts FATAL rather than silently no-op'ing when either anchor moves, that a
second application is a no-op, and -- via `--mutate`, which drops the trailing
flush -- that the equivalence comparison can actually go red.

`--upstream` re-fetches the live `ShellScriptlets.py` and asserts the recorded
copy still matches it, which is the one thing a recorded copy cannot tell you.


WHAT THE SPEEDUP ACTUALLY DEPENDS ON

The gain comes from consecutive entries sharing a directory, so it is worth
being explicit about the bound rather than quoting 81x as if it were universal.
The selftest therefore carries a second, adversarial fixture of 300 entries in
300 *different* directories, where no batching is structurally possible:

	ok   identical placement with every entry in its own directory
	ok   never slower than upstream: 903 -> 602 forks (1.5x)
	ok   the dirname saving survives even with no batching

1.5x -- the `dirname` saving alone. That is the floor, it is still a strict
improvement, and the assertion is that it is never slower rather than that it is
always 81x faster. Real entry lists cluster by directory, which is where the 81x
comes from.


ONE BEHAVIOUR DIFFERENCE, ON FAILING BUILDS ONLY

A batched `mv` reports a failure at different granularity than a per-file one.
Under `set -e`, upstream aborts on the exact entry that failed, with everything
before it already moved; the batched version aborts on the whole batch, so a
different subset of that directory's files will have moved before the abort.

This cannot affect a build that succeeds, and a build that fails here is thrown
away either way -- haikuporter does not resume a partially packaged subpackage.
It is recorded because the staging directory left behind after a failure is
sometimes inspected by hand, and it will look different.


RETIRE WHEN

haikuporter's own `packageEntries` stops forking per entry -- upstream has no
such change as of 2026-10-16. The patcher's idempotency marker makes it a no-op
once the anchors stop matching... except that a moved anchor is deliberately
FATAL, not skipped, so an upstream rewrite surfaces as a loud provisioning
failure and this note is what explains it. Delete the patcher, keep this file.
